# Rust coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern Rust
(2021/2024 edition) idiom. Applies only to `*.rs` files — everything not
Rust-specific still comes from the common rules this file `extends`.

## Naming and structure

- `snake_case` for functions, methods, variables, and modules;
  `UpperCamelCase` for types, traits, and enum variants; `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`
  for constants and statics — `rustfmt`/`rustc` will warn on a mismatch.
- A crate's public surface lives at the paths re-exported from `lib.rs`;
  keep implementation details `pub(crate)` or private and re-export
  deliberately, rather than making every module `pub` by default.
- An identifier's name shrinks with its scope: a short-lived closure
  parameter can be `x`, a `pub` function gets a full descriptive name.

## Ownership, borrowing, and errors

- Prefer borrowing (`&T`/`&mut T`) over taking ownership when the callee
  does not need to keep the value; prefer `&str`/`&[T]` parameters over
  `&String`/`&Vec<T>` so callers can pass either owned or borrowed data.
- Resolve a borrow-checker conflict by restructuring ownership, splitting
  the borrow, or shortening a borrow's lifetime — reach for `.clone()`
  only when the value genuinely needs to be duplicated, and say why in a
  comment when the clone is non-obvious; an unnecessary `.clone()` used
  purely to silence the borrow checker is a cost paid on every call.
- `Result<T, E>` for anything that can fail; propagate with the `?`
  operator rather than a manual `match` that just re-returns `Err`.
  `Option<T>` for genuine absence, not as a substitute for `Result` on an
  operation that can fail for a reason worth naming.
- Never call `.unwrap()`/`.expect()` on a `Result`/`Option` built from
  fallible or untrusted input (a parsed argument, a network response, a
  file read, a map lookup on external data) in production code without a
  stated invariant that makes it truly infallible; prefer `?`, a `match`,
  or a combinator (`unwrap_or`, `unwrap_or_else`, `ok_or`) that handles the
  failure path.
- Use `thiserror` to derive `std::error::Error` for a library's own error
  enum (`#[error("...")]` per variant, `#[source]`/`#[from]` to preserve
  the underlying cause); use `anyhow::Result`/`.context()`/`.with_context()`
  in application/binary code that just needs to propagate and annotate
  errors up to `main`, not to dictate a precise error type to its own
  callers.

## Traits and generics

- Accept the narrowest trait bound a function actually needs (`AsRef<str>`
  over `&String`, `Iterator<Item = T>` over `Vec<T>`) so callers keep
  flexibility in what they pass.
- Prefer returning a concrete type (`impl Trait` in return position) over
  a boxed trait object (`Box<dyn Trait>`) unless runtime polymorphism
  (a heterogeneous collection, a plugin-style callback list) is actually
  needed — `impl Trait` avoids the allocation and indirection.
- Derive `Debug`, `Clone`, `PartialEq`, etc. instead of hand-writing them
  when the type's fields support it; hand-write only when the derived
  behavior would be wrong for the type's invariants.

## Iterators and control flow

- Prefer an iterator chain (`.map`/`.filter`/`.fold`/`.collect`) over a
  hand-rolled indexed loop when it expresses the transformation as clearly
  — but do not force a chain that reads worse than a plain `for` loop just
  to look idiomatic; readability wins over either style has priority.
- Use pattern matching (`match`, `if let`, `let else`) to handle every
  variant of an enum explicitly; avoid a catch-all `_ =>` arm on a `match`
  over your own enum where a new variant should force every call site to
  be revisited.

## Async conventions (when the crate uses async)

- `async fn` for I/O-bound work; never call a blocking operation
  (`std::thread::sleep`, a synchronous file/socket read, a `std::sync`
  primitive held across an `.await` point) inside an `async fn` running on
  a multi-threaded executor — use the async equivalent (`tokio::time::sleep`,
  `tokio::fs`, `tokio::sync::Mutex`) or `tokio::task::spawn_blocking` for
  genuinely CPU-bound or blocking work.
- Keep a `Mutex` guard's scope as short as possible and never hold one
  across an `.await`; prefer `tokio::sync::Mutex` when the lock itself
  must be held across an await point, `std::sync::Mutex` when it never is.

## Formatting and lints

- Format with `rustfmt` (`cargo fmt`) before finishing a change; do not
  hand-format around a formatter that is already configured.
- Run `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` and fix findings at the root cause
  rather than adding a blanket `#[allow(...)]`.
